[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified local L2/L3 connectivity across Office A and found no fault. From PC1 (10.1.0.100) to PC2 (10.1.0.101), end-to-end pings succeed consistently: 10/10 received, 0% loss, sub‑2 ms RTT. PC1’s neighbor table shows 10.1.0.101 in REACHABLE state, confirming clean ARP resolution. The topology-wide reachability matrix also shows successful PC1→PC2 and PC2→PC1 connectivity.

On the access/distribution side, DSWA1’s VLAN interface for the Office A user VLAN (br0.10) is UP, and its lower interfaces (bond members/uplinks) are LOWER_UP, indicating no local switch/port failure. Uplinks from access switches are healthy: aswa2 eth1/eth2 and aswa3 eth1/eth2 all report “Link detected: yes” via ethtool, and DSWA1/DSWA2 peer-facing ports are up. These confirm the distribution and access path between PC1 (on aswa2) and PC2 (on aswa3) is operational.

Mechanism: If DSWA1 or an access port had failed, we would see ARP failures, ping loss, or interfaces DOWN; instead, ARP is valid and there is zero packet loss. Localization: no defective interfaces or daemons were observed on DSWA1, DSWA2, aswa2, or aswa3.

Root cause: No network-side fault currently present; the reported outage was transient or endpoint-specific and has recovered.

Corrective fix: No device or link restart is recommended. If the issue recurs, check endpoint firewalls/NIC power states and clear ARP on the hosts; also capture during the event (ping/arp) to isolate a potential host-side transient.
